markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Filtering

#### Imagine opening a music app and seeing a playlist labeled “Top 10 Songs Right Now.” Only ten songs appear even though thousands of songs exist on the platform.

<br>

<img src="https://images.code.org/87e2e5cfc91d0d45db89936e0818a5d1-image-1767631501751.png" alt="example list of top 10 songs" width="400px" style= "border-radius:10px; float:right; margin: 0 10px 10px 20px;">

<img src="https://images.code.org/59c9b8a356ff4e56ec8c802ba5718b37-image-1684269154206.png" alt="A clipboard with the words TO DO on it. This icon is used to signal an action item for participants." width="30px" style="border-radius:10px; float:left; margin: 0px 10px 10px 0px;">

#### **Pause & Reflect**

- #### If an app only shows songs from the Top 10, what happens to everyone else?

- #### What rule do you think the app uses to decide which songs appear?

- #### What is one change you could make to that rule to make the playlist feel more fair or inclusive?

<br>

> #### You may have considered things like: someone decided this rule, some data is included, some is excluded, newer, older, or less mainstream artists may be left out. 

#### 👉 This playlist didn’t happen by accident. It was created using a **filtering algorithm**.
---

## What is a filter algorithm? 

#### A **filter algorithm** is a rule a program uses to look through a list of data and keep only the items that meet certain conditions.

#### In JavaScript, filtering is often used with arrays of objects to narrow a large list into a smaller, more relevant one like deciding which songs, messages, or results appear in a web app.

<br>

  <div style="text-align: center; width: 100%;">

  <div style="display: inline-block; background-color: #efe7f8; border: 2px solid #8c52ba; border-radius: 12px; padding: 20px 30px; max-width: 700px; box-shadow: 0 2px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <p style="font-size: 1.2rem;  margin: 0; line-height: 1.5; color: #3b0066;">
    Every filter starts as a <b>condition</b>. <br>Every condition is a <b>choice</b> that changes what is visible and what is hidden.
    </p>
  </div>

</div>


